    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\int {(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To compute the integral of a general exponential function, we just need to divide by the natural logarithm of the base of the exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}}{ln⁡(\\frac{1}{8})}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the laws of logarithms, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ln⁡(\\frac{1}{8}) & =ln⁡1-ln⁡8 \\\\  & =0-ln⁡8 \\\\  & =-ln⁡8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can simplify our final answer as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}}{ln⁡(\\frac{1}{8})}+C=-\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}}{ln⁡8}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/id": "c9333ab3-e125-5f05-9da5-ff282fd92c31",
